\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The reversed top-word chart}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Choose an ordered basis #{\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_N} of #{V^*} in
\ref{fdip-008F}. Multiplication is written by juxtaposition, so
##{\xi_i\xi_j=-\xi_j\xi_i,\qquad \xi_i^2=0.}
The monomials #{1} and #{\xi_{i_1}\cdots\xi_{i_r}} with
#{i_1<\cdots<i_r} form a basis. Take the reversed top word
#{\omega=\xi_N\cdots\xi_1} as the supplied volume. Its Berezin
integral is
##{I_\xi(f)=\text{coefficient of }\xi_N\cdots\xi_1
       \text{ in }f,}
so #{I_\xi(\xi_N\cdots\xi_1)=1}. Relative to the increasing top
word #{\xi_1\cdots\xi_N}, this volume has the sign
#{(-1)^{N(N-1)/2}}. For #{N=0} the functional is the identity on
#{\mathbb k}. This is the purely odd coefficient definition of
\citet{v2, Definition 9.6, p. 128}{etingof2024mathematical},
with the top order stated explicitly. The ordered monomial basis
is supplied formally by
\leanref{Module.Basis.ExteriorAlgebra}; this supports the
algebraic coordinates, not the Berezin or Gaussian identities.}
